A||N||15||jenstewy||back firing||||5||
Z||000000||jenstewy||07-09-2000||05:22 PM||||I have a 88 four cylinder automatic with 53000 miles.  A few weeks ago the car started backfiring under load and idling rough.<P>So far I have replaced the plugs, the plug wires, and the O2 sensor.  The check engine light is not on.  There are no obvious vacuum leaks.  A compression check was excellent.  Removing the connector from the MAP sensor had little effect except to turn on the check engine light.<P>Problem seems to occur when its hot and humid (engine dry).<P>HELP!!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Philphine||07-09-2000||05:56 PM||||just posted the same problem on my '87 4cyl. i changed plugs and wires, idle speed control (after getting a code for it),and air and fuel filters. i did these things trying to track down a different problem that seems to be fixed but now i have this backfiring and sputter, too. just on a guess, i'm thinking maybe i'm not getting enough fuel. i'm about to go and try to get an in line fuel pressure guage. i'll tell you what if anything i find out and watch both posts.<P>------------------<BR><P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||07-09-2000||06:10 PM||||I don't see how a fuel problem will cause backfiring. Most of the time backfiring is caused by an ignition problem, bad valve(s), or if the timing is off. Since the 87 and 88 four cylinder engines don't have distributors, that eliminates a lot of problems. Have the coil packs tested and triple check those plug wires.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||07-09-2000||06:18 PM||||i have an 84 fiero, and mine would backfire, use to, when the spark plug wires would crack from the heat. Try bending the wires slightly to see any cracks, and also check if the wires are actually on the connections||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||07-09-2000||06:32 PM||||Sounds like the ignition module to me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||07-09-2000||11:09 PM||||Do the DIS 2.5 motors have a ignition module? We need Ogre for this answer. I don't know for sure, but I thought the DIS engines had a crank sensor instead. I might be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DiggityDan||07-10-2000||12:10 AM||||Have you checked the vacuum advance?<BR>I know on my 85 Turbo Conquest this will cause a similar problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DiggityDan||07-10-2000||12:15 AM||||Nevermind......DIS...no distributor......no vacuum advance||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mattsfiero||07-10-2000||05:30 AM||||Is there a problem code?......||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phantom Rage||07-10-2000||06:17 AM||||Check to see if your tbi hold down bolts are tight.  If they are loose, tighten them to see if it helps any.  If so, you should either get a new gasket or rebuild your tbi. I had two serious backfires one day; I even had to get my poor car towed back home.  I got a rebuild kit form Autozone, about $25.00 or so (cake job.)  After the rebuild, it took about 1 min before the car ran right.  A bit of Loc tight wouldn't hurt either.  Also check to see if your intake manifold bolts are tight <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mbramble||07-10-2000||07:13 AM||||Sounds VERY similar to the problems I was having with my 87 Duke -- and they only got worse.  Mine was caused by a bad coil pack.  Mine was heat sensitive so the symptoms being described only occurred after the car warmed up.  Pontiac dealer diagnosed it in a few minutes, replaced it, ran thru some de-carbonizer stuff because of all the stuff that builds with all that poor combustion and backfiring and it has been running beautifully every since.....<P>(knocks on wood!!)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phoenix||07-10-2000||09:42 AM||||Ditto - I have an 88 coupe that had the same backfire problem with hard starting. It also was a bad coil.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lennyb4||07-10-2000||09:52 AM||||Hey batboy... Yes the DIS motors do have a ignition module. The ignition coils are attatched to this (actually they mount right on top of it) by two electronic leads and mounting screws. On the 2.5 the coil packs and control module are located on the side of the engine block about 1/2 way down directly below the intake.||||reg||14
Z||000013||jenstewy||07-10-2000||10:09 PM||||Thanks for everyone's help!  I took it to the dealer today and asked them to check the coils and to change the fuel filter (had never been changed).<P>They replaced the filter and coil ASM part number 10472401.  Total cost $155(parts and labor).<P>Its running alot better, more as I put some more miles on it....<P>Thanks again!||||reg||10
Z||000014||theogre||07-10-2000||10:25 PM||||You also want to check the MAT and MAP sensors when you have a problem like this. Even w/o a code in the ECM.(Yes, 87-88 4 cyls have MAT)<P>1. the MAT sensor is/was likely cooked if the backfire was into the intake. take it out and check it at the very least. The sensor inside the cage should not touch anything, be covered in dirt/carbon, or otherwise cooked looking. (MAT has a much bigger effect on the 4 than on the v6. Has allot to do with location of the sensor.)<P>2. MAP vacuum line that's too long, blocked, or colapsing, can also cause such things.<P>if the ignition brick has been removed for any reason, keep an eye on it for oil leaks. Most service persons fail to tape the threads of the bolts that hold it to the block. If it leaks, take the bolts out one at a time, teflon tape them, and put them back in. that should seal it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||theogre||07-10-2000||10:28 PM||||P.S. always always always check the ECM for codes even if you haven't seen the light come on. This is especially important on the DIS motors where the computer totally controls everything.||||reg||1
